[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So V6 is here and we\'re really excited to introduce our most powerful generation of models yet. All three new V6 models were built in partnership with artists and the music industry with a huge jump in audio quality control and what you can actually ask to do. You can'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'combine multiple songs and references into a single request. Turn photos, videos, and voice memos into music. Make really specific changes to songs you\'ve already created. and give Sunoa much'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more detailed creative control than before. There are three new models in this generation. But before we jump into those workflows, let\'s quickly talk about what each one is for. V6 is our flagship model. It\'s our most reliable'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and highest quality model yet. It\'s the one that I\'d reach for when I have a clear idea for what I want. V6 Wild is for exploration. It creates more diversity between song outputs and gives'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your ideas more room to go in different directions. So, it\'s useful when you want to experiment and find something you might not have thought to ask for. If you\'re someone who loved the creativity, personality, and variation'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of Sunno\'s older models, V6 Wild is probably the first model I\'d try, and V6 Mini is the new model available to free users. It\'s faster and more efficient and a significant upgrade over the'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'previous free model. One of the biggest changes with V6 is how much more creative direction you can give pseudo at once. Previously, what you wanted to accomplish often determined which tool you needed to reach for. You might use'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one workflow for cover, another for sampling, another for a voice reference, another for working with stems. Now, in simple mode, you can start with what you\'re actually trying to make. You can'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'describe the result in plain language, attach the material you want Sununo to work with, and even include several different instructions in the same request. V6 can interpret all of those pieces together and use them as part of'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the direction for the song. The best way for me to explain this is just to show you. Let\'s start with one of my favorite examples because this is where you can really see how much more information V6 can handle in a single request. I\'m'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to attach a couple of songs and say take the vocals from the first song &gt;&gt; and the drums from the second song'

[02:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; and add new lyrics about starting over and turn the result into an 80s synth'

[02:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wave. song. And now let\'s listen to what it made again. Start again.'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'By doing this, I\'m pointing V6 towards specific elements from different songs, asking it to combine them, giving it a'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'new lyrical direction, and changing the overall musical style at the same time. And you can get much more specific about what you want from each reference. So, for example, if I was to write, use the'

[03:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'driving rhythm from the first song, the atmosphere from the second song, and the vocal energy from the third song to create a new track. Run with me where the rest turn green.'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Leave a lot of rooms and they almost spin. It\'s the roads hard. Let it carry us through. I\'m not looking back when I\'m running with you.'

[03:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; The more specific I get about what I actually like in each reference, the more information V6 has to work with. That\'s one of the biggest differences with V6. You can give Suno a lot more creative direction at once instead of'

[04:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'breaking the idea into a bunch of separate steps. And I can even get more specific here. Let\'s say there\'s a guitar part in this song that I want to use somewhere else. Let\'s first listen to the guitar part.'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And now I can write sample the guitar riff at 45 seconds. Isolate the guitar'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and build a new beat around it. Normally, those are several different instructions. Find this specific part, isolate the instrument, then use it as a basis for something else. Here, I\'m just'

[04:46] [scene:talk]
